/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Thevenin's Theorem and provide a rigorous step-by-step mathematical derivation of its equivalent parameters.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Thevenin's Theorem: Formulation and Analytical Proof:) Tj
T*
(   1. Formal Statement:) Tj
T*
(   Any linear, active, bilateral resistive network with terminals A and B can be replaced by an) Tj
T*
(   equivalent circuit consisting of a single independent voltage source Vth in series with an) Tj
T*
(   equivalent resistance Rth.) Tj
T*
(   - Vth \(Thevenin Voltage\): The open-circuit voltage across terminals A-B when the load resistor) Tj
T*
(   RL is disconnected: Vth = V_oc.) Tj
T*
(   - Rth \(Thevenin Resistance\): The equivalent driving-point resistance seen looking into) Tj
T*
(   terminals A-B with all independent sources deactivated \(voltage sources short-circuited,) Tj
T*
(   current sources open-circuited\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Derivation via Superposition:) Tj
T*
(   - Connect a variable load resistance RL across terminals A-B carrying load current I_L.) Tj
T*
(   - By substitution theorem, replace RL with an opposing ideal voltage source V_L = I_L * RL such) Tj
T*
(   that the net current in the circuit remains unchanged.) Tj
T*
(   - By Superposition Theorem, the load current I_L is the sum of currents produced by two) Tj
T*
(   distinct sub-circuits:) Tj
T*
(   Sub-circuit 1: All internal independent sources are ACTIVE, while the opposing load voltage) Tj
T*
(   source V_L is set to 0 \(short circuit\). Let the resulting short-circuit current flowing out of) Tj
T*
(   terminal A be I_sc.) Tj
T*
(   Sub-circuit 2: All internal independent sources are DEACTIVATED \(set to 0\), and the opposing) Tj
T*
