(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Norton's Theorem, compare it with Thevenin's Theorem, and demonstrate source transformation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Norton's Theorem and Source Transformation:) Tj
T*
(   1. Norton's Theorem Statement:) Tj
T*
(   Any linear active resistive network connected to output terminals A and B can be completely) Tj
T*
(   replaced by an equivalent current source In in parallel with an equivalent resistance Rn.) Tj
T*
(   - In \(Norton Current\): Short-circuit current flowing between A and B when terminals are) Tj
T*
(   shorted: In = I_sc.) Tj
T*
(   - Rn \(Norton Resistance\): Resistance seen between A and B with all independent sources zeroed.) Tj
T*
(   Rn = Rth.) Tj
T*
(   2. Duality and Direct Equivalence:) Tj
T*
(   - Thevenin circuit: V = Vth - I_L * Rth.) Tj
T*
(   - Norton circuit: I_L = In - V / Rn => V = In * Rn - I_L * Rn.) Tj
T*
(   - Equating coefficients reveals:) Tj
T*
(   Rn = Rth, and Vth = In * Rn = In * Rth.) Tj
T*
(   3. Source Transformation Technique:) Tj
T*
(   - A practical voltage source \(V_s in series with R_s\) is mathematically and externally) Tj
T*
(   indistinguishable from a practical current source \(I_s in parallel with R_p\) if:) Tj
T*
(   I_s = V_s / R_s and R_p = R_s.) Tj
T*
(   - Conversely: V_s = I_s * R_p with series resistance R_s = R_p.) Tj
T*
(   - Source transformation simplifies complex ladder networks by iteratively transforming parallel) Tj
T*
(   current branches into series voltage branches.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
