(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Mesh Analysis methodology, matrix formulation, and handling of supermeshes containing current sources.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Mesh Analysis and Supermesh Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   1. Systematic Procedure for Planar Networks:) Tj
T*
(   - Identify M = B - N + 1 independent meshes.) Tj
T*
(   - Assign clockwise circulating mesh currents I1, I2, ..., Im to each mesh.) Tj
T*
(   - Apply KVL around each mesh loop: Sum of voltage drops = 0.) Tj
T*
(   2. Matrix Formulation \([R] * [I] = [V]\):) Tj
T*
(   - [R] is an M x M resistance matrix:) Tj
T*
(   * Diagonal entries R_ii: Self-resistance \(sum of all resistances around mesh i\).) Tj
T*
(   * Off-diagonal entries R_ij: Mutual resistance \(-sum of resistances shared between mesh i and) Tj
T*
(   mesh j\).) Tj
T*
(   - [I] is the vector of unknown mesh currents [I1, I2, ..., Im]^T.) Tj
T*
(   - [V] is the algebraic sum of independent voltage rises encountered traversing mesh i in) Tj
T*
(   direction of I_i.) Tj
T*
(   3. Supermesh Construction:) Tj
T*
(   - When an independent or dependent current source lies on a branch shared by two meshes \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(   mesh 1 and mesh 2\), the voltage across the current source is unknown.) Tj
T*
(   - Create a Supermesh by virtually removing the shared branch containing the current source) Tj
T*
(   \(open-circuiting it\), forming a merged single loop.) Tj
T*
(   - Write a KVL equation around the perimeter of the supermesh.) Tj
T*
(   - Add a constraint equation linking the two mesh currents to the current source: I2 - I1 =) Tj
T*
(   I_source.) Tj
T*
