(   [Problem Statement]: State the Reciprocity Theorem, define its validity conditions, and illustrate with a two-port resistive network.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Reciprocity Theorem in Linear Bilateral Networks:) Tj
T*
(   1. Statement:) Tj
T*
(   In any linear, bilateral, time-invariant passive network, if an ideal voltage source V applied) Tj
T*
(   in branch 'j' produces a current I in branch 'k', then the same voltage source V placed in) Tj
T*
(   branch 'k' will produce the identical current I in branch 'j':) Tj
T*
(   \(I_k / V_j\) = \(I_j / V_k\), or Transfer Admittance Y_kj = Y_jk.) Tj
T*
(   2. Dual Current-Voltage Statement:) Tj
T*
(   If an ideal current source I injected at node pair 'j' produces an open-circuit voltage V) Tj
T*
(   across node pair 'k', then moving the current source I to node pair 'k' produces the identical) Tj
T*
(   voltage V across node pair 'j':) Tj
T*
(   \(V_k / I_j\) = \(V_j / I_k\), or Transfer Impedance Z_kj = Z_jk.) Tj
T*
(   3. Conditions and Restrictions:) Tj
T*
(   - Network must be linear \(no diodes, saturation, or non-linear elements\).) Tj
T*
(   - Elements must be bilateral \(resistors, inductors, capacitors\).) Tj
T*
(   - Network must contain NO dependent sources \(transistors, op-amps\) or non-reciprocal components) Tj
T*
(   \(gyrators, circulators\).) Tj
T*
(   - Initial stored energy must be zero.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
