(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the Loading Effect in voltmeter and ammeter measurements, deriving correction factors for true values.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Loading Effect in Electrical Instrumentation:) Tj
T*
(   1. Voltmeter Loading Effect:) Tj
T*
(   - An ideal voltmeter has infinite internal resistance \(R_v = infinity\).) Tj
T*
(   - A practical voltmeter has finite internal resistance R_v.) Tj
T*
(   - When connected across resistor R1 in parallel, equivalent resistance drops to R_parallel =) Tj
T*
(   \(R1 * R_v\) / \(R1 + R_v\) < R1.) Tj
T*
(   - This reduces the effective circuit resistance and causes the measured voltage V_m to be) Tj
T*
(   strictly less than the true open-circuit voltage V_true:) Tj
T*
(   V_m = V_true * [R_v / \(R_th + R_v\)], where R_th is the Thevenin resistance seen across the) Tj
T*
(   measurement nodes.) Tj
T*
(   - Percentage Error: % Error = [\(V_m - V_true\) / V_true] * 100% = - [R_th / \(R_th + R_v\)] *) Tj
T*
(   100%.) Tj
T*
(   - To keep error < 1%, R_v must be > 100 * R_th.) Tj
T*
(   2. Ammeter Loading Effect:) Tj
T*
(   - An ideal ammeter has zero internal resistance \(R_a = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - A practical ammeter inserts a small internal resistance R_a in series with the branch.) Tj
T*
(   - Total loop resistance increases: R_new = R_th + R_a.) Tj
T*
(   - Measured current: I_m = V_th / \(R_th + R_a\) < I_true.) Tj
T*
(   - Percentage Error: % Error = - [R_a / \(R_th + R_a\)] * 100%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
