(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the concept of Supermesh in detail with a complete worked mathematical example.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Supermesh Analysis: Complete Theoretical and Numerical Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Concept:) Tj
T*
(   - A supermesh is created when two adjacent meshes share an independent or dependent current) Tj
T*
(   source. Because the voltage drop across a current source cannot be expressed using Ohm's law,) Tj
T*
(   the branch containing the current source is bypassed, creating a combined larger loop.) Tj
T*
(   2. Worked Numerical Problem:) Tj
T*
(   - Two meshes: Mesh 1 has current I1; Mesh 2 has current I2 \(both clockwise\).) Tj
T*
(   - Mesh 1 has 20 V voltage source on left branch, 4 Ohm resistor in mesh 1 only.) Tj
T*
(   - The shared central branch contains an independent current source of 6 A directed upward.) Tj
T*
(   - Mesh 2 has an 8 Ohm resistor on right branch connected to ground.) Tj
T*
(   3. Equations:) Tj
T*
(   - Constraint Equation: The shared branch carries current source 6 A upward. Mesh current I2) Tj
T*
(   flows upward through it, while I1 flows downward:) Tj
T*
(   I2 - I1 = 6 => I2 = I1 + 6.) Tj
T*
(   - Supermesh KVL Equation: Traverse the outer boundary bypassing the current source:) Tj
T*
(   -20 + 4 * I1 + 8 * I2 = 0 => 4 * I1 + 8 * I2 = 20.) Tj
T*
(   4. Solution:) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute I2: 4 * I1 + 8 * \(I1 + 6\) = 20 => 4 * I1 + 8 * I1 + 48 = 20 => 12 * I1 = -28.) Tj
T*
(   - I1 = -28 / 12 = -2.333 A.) Tj
T*
(   - I2 = -2.333 + 6 = +3.667 A.) Tj
T*
(   - Current through 8 Ohm resistor = 3.667 A \(downward\); voltage across 8 Ohm resistor = 29.33 V.) Tj
T*
