(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Star-Delta equivalence in terms of multi-terminal networks and driving-point conductance.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Generalized Star-Delta Equivalence and Multi-Terminal Systems:) Tj
T*
(   1. Generalized Node Elimination:) Tj
T*
(   - Delta-Star conversion is a specific case of Kron reduction \(Gaussian elimination of an) Tj
T*
(   internal node\).) Tj
T*
(   - Consider a star network of n branches radiating from internal node 0 to external terminals 1,) Tj
T*
(   2, ..., n.) Tj
T*
(   - Applying KCL at internal node 0:) Tj
T*
(   Sum_{k=1}^n \(V0 - Vk\) * G_k = 0 => V0 = [Sum_{k=1}^n Vk * G_k] / [Sum_{k=1}^n G_k].) Tj
T*
(   - Current entering terminal j:) Tj
T*
(   I_j = \(Vj - V0\) * G_j = Vj * G_j - G_j * [Sum_{k=1}^n Vk * G_k] / Sum\(G\).) Tj
T*
(   - Rewriting in terms of direct pairwise delta conductances between terminal j and terminal k:) Tj
T*
(   G_jk = \(G_j * G_k\) / Sum_{m=1}^n G_m.) Tj
T*
(   2. Preservation of External Equivalence:) Tj
T*
(   - External driving-point conductances and transfer impedances between any pair of terminals) Tj
T*
(   remain identical.) Tj
T*
(   - For n = 3, this yields the standard 3-terminal Star-Delta conversion formulas.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
