12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Gyrator circuit, its impedance inversion properties, and how it synthesizes inductors using capacitors.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Gyrator and Inductor Synthesis:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Characterization:) Tj
T*
(   - An ideal gyrator is a linear, lossless, non-reciprocal two-port network defined by gyration) Tj
T*
(   resistance 'r':) Tj
T*
(   v1 = -r * i2) Tj
T*
(   v2 = +r * i1) Tj
T*
(   - Two-port admittance matrix:) Tj
T*
(   [Y] = [ 0 1/r ]) Tj
T*
(   [ -1/r 0 ].) Tj
T*
(   - Notice [Y] is skew-symmetric, confirming it is non-reciprocal and dissipates zero net power:) Tj
T*
(   P = v1*i1 + v2*i2 = \(-r*i2\)*i1 + \(r*i1\)*i2 = 0.) Tj
T*
(   2. Impedance Inversion:) Tj
T*
(   - Connect an impedance Z_L across port 2: v2 = -i2 * Z_L => i2 = -v2 / Z_L.) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute into port 1 equation:) Tj
T*
(   v1 = -r * \(-v2 / Z_L\) = \(r / Z_L\) * \(r * i1\) = \(r^2 / Z_L\) * i1.) Tj
T*
(   - Input Impedance: Z_in = v1 / i1 = r^2 / Z_L.) Tj
T*
