(   [Problem Statement]: Demonstrate Source Shifting \(Blakesley's Theorem\) for voltage sources and current sources.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Source Shifting Techniques \(Blakesley's Theorem\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Voltage Source Shifting:) Tj
T*
(   - An ideal voltage source V connected in a branch between nodes A and B can be shifted through) Tj
T*
(   node A into all other branches connected to node A.) Tj
T*
(   - Procedure: Remove the voltage source from the original branch, and insert an identical) Tj
T*
(   voltage source V into every other branch radiating from node A, with identical polarity toward) Tj
T*
(   node A.) Tj
T*
(   - Validity: All branch voltages and node potential differences throughout the entire remainder) Tj
T*
(   of the network remain strictly unchanged.) Tj
T*
(   2. Current Source Shifting:) Tj
T*
(   - An ideal current source I connected across nodes A and B can be replaced by two identical) Tj
T*
(   current sources in series through an intermediate node C.) Tj
T*
(   - Or, a current source across two non-adjacent nodes can be shifted across loops without) Tj
T*
(   altering terminal KCL equations.) Tj
T*
(   3. Application:) Tj
T*
(   - Eliminates floating voltage sources to facilitate straightforward standard Nodal Analysis) Tj
T*
(   without creating supernodes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
