(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Power Distribution in a 3-Wire DC Distribution System, comparing copper efficiency with a 2-Wire system.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   3-Wire DC Distribution System and Copper Efficiency:) Tj
T*
(   1. System Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Composed of two outer conductors \(+V and -V relative to neutral\) and a central neutral wire) Tj
T*
(   \(0 V\) grounded at the station.) Tj
T*
(   - Standard voltages: 440 V between outers for industrial motive power; 220 V between either) Tj
T*
(   outer and neutral for lighting and domestic loads.) Tj
T*
(   - Balanced loads draw equal currents from positive and negative outers; neutral current is zero) Tj
T*
(   \(I_N = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Under unbalance: I_N = I_pos - I_neg.) Tj
T*
(   2. Copper Volume Comparison \(3-Wire vs 2-Wire System\):) Tj
T*
(   - Let both systems transmit total power P over distance L with identical maximum voltage to) Tj
T*
(   ground V and identical power loss P_loss.) Tj
T*
(   - 2-Wire System:) Tj
T*
(   Transmits power at voltage V. Current I1 = P / V.) Tj
T*
(   P_loss = 2 * I1^2 * R1 = 2 * \(P/V\)^2 * \(rho * L / a1\).) Tj
T*
(   Volume of copper: Vol_2 = 2 * a1 * L.) Tj
T*
(   - 3-Wire System:) Tj
T*
(   Transmits power between outers at voltage 2V. Current in each outer I2 = P / \(2V\) = I1 / 2.) Tj
T*
(   Assuming balanced load \(neutral carries negligible current\), neutral cross-section is typically) Tj
T*
